    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Wed, Aug 24, 2011 at 7:35 PM, David Kahle <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">david...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\n<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);margin:0pt 0pt 0pt 0.8ex;padding-left:1ex\"><div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div>Hi Swagath -<br><br>Something like this should do the trick....<br>\n<br>df &lt;- read.csv(file.choose())<br><br>ggplot(aes(x = Lon, y = Lat, fill = Value), data = df) +<br> geom_point() # so the points are there...<br><br>ggplot(aes(x = Lon, y = Lat, fill = Value), data = df) +<br> geom_tile() # the bad plot you&#39;re getting<br>\n<br># make latitude maxes (uniform, so easy)<br>diff(sort(df$Lat))<br>df &lt;- transform(df, latmax = Lat + resolution(Lat))<br><br># make latitude maxes (nonuniform, so a little more complicated)<br>diff(sort(df$Lon))<br>\nqplot(diff(sort(Lon)), data = df, geom = &#39;density&#39;)<br>df &lt;- ddply(df, .(Lat), function(df){<br> transform(df, lonmax = Lon + min(resolution(Lon), .2))<br>})<br><br># plot<br>ggplot(aes(fill = Value), data = df) +<br>\n geom_rect(aes(xmin = Lon, xmax = lonmax, ymin = Lat, ymax = latmax))<br><br><br>Cheers<br>david.</div><div><br></div><br></div><div><div><div>On Aug 24, 2011, at 11:07 AM, Swagath Navin wrote:</div><br></div><blockquote type=\"cite\">\n<div><div><div></div><div>Dear list members,<br><br>I am trying to replicate a spatial plot (sample1.pdf) with Lat, Lon and corresponding values in ggplot2 using geom_tile. However, the size of cells is different. How i can adjust the size of the cells, to make it look like the plot in sample.pdf.<br>\n<br>Here the code i used:<br><br>dum = read.csv(&#39;1.csv&#39;, header = TRUE, sep=&#39;,&#39;)<br>dum$Lat&lt;-round(dum$Lat, digits=2)<br>dum$Lon&lt;-round(dum$Lon, digits=2)<br>dum$Value&lt;-round(dum$Value, digits=2)<br>\n<br>ggplot(dum, aes(x=Lon, y=Lat))+geom_tile(aes(fill=Value))<br><br>In case if you 